				"\"A close-up, slow-motion shot of a clear glass cup, half-filled with water, placed at the edge of a wooden dining table. The camera focuses on the cup as it begins to wobble slightly, then tips over and falls toward the floor. The descent is captured in dramatic slow motion, showing the water starting to spill out in shimmering droplets.\nThe cup hits the hard floor with a sharp 'Crack!'​ — the initial impact sound is crisp and high-pitched. This is immediately followed by a series of rapid, sharper cracking and tinkling sounds as the glass fractures into large pieces (\"Pīlī-pālā\"). The camera then zooms in on the debris: shards of glass skitter across the floor, producing lighter, chaotic 'cling-clang'​ and scraping noises, while the remaining water splashes softly. Smaller fragments continue to settle with faint, delicate 'ting'​ sounds, until the audio fades to silence.\nThroughout the sequence, the visual and auditory elements are tightly synchronized: the initial 'Crack' aligns precisely with the frame of impact; the shattering noises match the rapid fragmentation​ of the glass; and the final lighter sounds coincide with the skittering and settling of the shards. The lighting is natural, coming from a side window, highlighting the transparency and sharp edges of the broken glass.\""
